# Campaign and methodology documentation — PROPRIETARY, moved 2026-08-04.
# The source in this repository is public (GPL v3); the knowledge of how it was built
# is not. These records now live in the PRIVATE spert-suite-docs repository:
#   suite/                  the practices — one copy, suite-wide
#   apps/scheduler/         this repo's charter, close-outs, baselines, censuses
# Ignored rather than merely removed so they cannot be re-added by habit.
docs/

# Working notes at the repository root — analyses, plans, critiques, briefings,
# orchestration prompts. Same proprietary-methodology concern as `docs/` above,
# arriving by a different route: these are never deliberately added, they are
# swept up.
#
# ⚠️ THIS IS A SAFETY PATTERN, NOT TIDINESS. A root-level planning document that
# is untracked but NOT ignored is one `git add -A` away from a public commit.
# That has already happened twice in spert-forecaster, caught both times only
# because someone read the file list before pushing. A near-miss caught twice by
# attention is a hazard, not a track record.
#
# Measured 2026-08-11, before this block existed: 108 such files sat in this
# repository's root and `git check-ignore` matched exactly 0 of them.
#
# ⚠️ DENY-BY-DEFAULT WITH AN ALLOWLIST — deliberately NOT a vocabulary of
# filename patterns. spert-forecaster solved this in its #168 with patterns
# scoped to its own naming style (`/*-CRITIQUE-*.md`, `/*-PLAN-V*.md`, …).
# That block was measured against this repo's 108 real filenames and catches
# FOUR: Forecaster writes `NAME-CRITIQUE-v2.md`, this repo writes
# `CRITIQUE_name.md`. It appears to catch 16 when tested on macOS, but 12 of
# those are an APFS case-folding artifact (`-PLAN-V*` matching `-plan-v3`) that
# vanishes on any case-sensitive checkout — which is what CI and cloud agents
# run. A copy-paste rollout would have looked like a fix and fired on almost
# nothing.
#
# Forecaster's own comment rejects the allowlist, reasoning that "a file missing
# from a commit is harder to notice than a file wrongly present in one." That is
# true in general and backwards here, because the two errors are not
# symmetrical in a public repo carrying private methodology:
#
#   - A doc wrongly ignored is visible on GitHub and fixed by one commit. And it
#     is not silent: `git add CONTRIBUTING.md` on an ignored path exits 1 and
#     prints a hint. Only `git add -A` passes over it quietly.
#   - A planning doc wrongly committed to a public repo is not recoverable. It
#     is in the history, cloneable and indexable.
#
# An allowlist is complete by construction; a vocabulary is incomplete by
# construction — Forecaster's comment concedes as much ("a planning doc with
# novel naming is still untracked-and-unignored").
#
# ⚠️ `.gitignore` has no effect on already-tracked files, so `/*.md` does not
# untrack anything listed below and never could. The allowlist is here so that
# creating a NEW SECURITY.md or CONTRIBUTING.md is still a plain `git add`, and
# so a reader can see at a glance what this repository means to publish.
#
# Derive the allowlist, never recall it:   git ls-files ':(glob)*.md'
/*.md
!/README.md
!/ARCHITECTURE.md
!/CHANGELOG.md
!/SECURITY.md
# Conventional root docs, not present today; listed so adding one needs no edit here.
!/CONTRIBUTING.md
!/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md
